WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.698 --> 00:00:15.631
"There is no greatness where simplicity, goodness, and truth are absent," wrote the Russian writer Leo Tolstoy.

2
00:00:15.632 --> 00:00:21.740
With these few words, he expressed a belief that would inspire him for much of his life.

3
00:00:21.741 --> 00:00:38.948
Tolstoy gave the world masterpieces such as War and Peace, an epic novel about Napoleon's invasion of Russia; Anna Karenina, a tragic tale of forbidden love; and The Death of Ivan Ilyich, a story of spiritual awakening and physical death.

4
00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:49.061
But despite his success, Leo Tolstoy decided in the end to leave his comfortable life behind to live as a simple peasant.

5
00:00:49.062 --> 00:01:01.718
Leo Tolstoy was born on August 28, 1828, at Yasnaya Polyana, his father's large country estate located 210 kilometers south of Moscow.

6
00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:09.644
Tolstoy's mother died when he was still an infant, and he became an orphan at age nine when his father died as well.

7
00:01:09.645 --> 00:01:17.919
Tolstoy and his four siblings lived with one relative after another until they settled at the home of a distant cousin.

8
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:27.182
After several years of studying with private tutors, Tolstoy enrolled at a university in western Russia when he was 16.

9
00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:35.167
But he was more interested in going to parties, and he eventually abandoned his schoolwork in favor of drinking and gambling.

10
00:01:35.168 --> 00:01:43.612
In 1847, nearly penniless, 19-year-old Leo Tolstoy returned home to manage the family estate.

11
00:01:43.613 --> 00:01:53.450
Unfortunately, the family home had been sold to pay his gambling debts, and Tolstoy had to live in a nearby caretaker's cottage.

12
00:01:53.451 --> 00:02:06.257
On the estate, Tolstoy supervised the serfs—peasants who were forced to work for wealthy families—and decided to educate himself by reading classic works of literature and philosophy.

13
00:02:06.258 --> 00:02:16.242
Despite his newfound desire for wisdom and maturity, his frequent visits to Moscow offered temptations he found hard to resist.

14
00:02:16.243 --> 00:02:21.035
He drank, gambled, and had many brief affairs with women.

15
00:02:21.036 --> 00:02:34.291
"Girls, silly music . . . heat, cigarette smoke, girls, vodka, cheese, screams and shouts, girls, girls, girls!" he noted in the diary he had started to track his self-improvement.

16
00:02:34.292 --> 00:02:43.738
Keeping a diary became a lifelong habit, and Tolstoy regularly recorded both his actions and his judgments about his actions in his diary.

17
00:02:43.739 --> 00:02:52.517
If he had gambled the day before, he would confess it on paper, scold himself, and then make a rule to correct his behavior.

18
00:02:52.518 --> 00:02:57.564
His efforts at self-discipline, however, were not very successful.

19
00:02:57.565 --> 00:03:02.759
"I am not pleased with myself," he wrote in his diary in 1851.

20
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:13.151
Frustrated with his wasteful ways and moral lapses, Tolstoy decided to join an artillery regiment and traveled south to the Caucasus Mountains.

21
00:03:13.152 --> 00:03:20.270
There, he recorded keen observations of military life in his diary and began writing stories.

22
00:03:20.271 --> 00:03:31.368
In 1852, Tolstoy sent a manuscript about his early years, eventually titled Childhood, to the literary journal The Contemporary, which published it.

23
00:03:31.369 --> 00:03:39.908
The Contemporary also published his short stories based on his experiences in the Caucasus, most notably "The Raid."

24
00:03:39.909 --> 00:03:43.851
His work was immediately noticed and widely praised.

25
00:03:43.852 --> 00:03:51.224
"His talent seemed geniuslike, and we were a little timid before it," wrote a reviewer in 1861.

26
00:03:51.225 --> 00:04:02.385
The Crimean War began in 1853, and Tolstoy, stationed in Sevastopol, a port city on the Black Sea, saw firsthand the brutality of war.

27
00:04:02.386 --> 00:04:13.820
He wrote in his book, Sevastopol, in 1856, "In the blooming valley . . . lay disfigured corpses, shoeless, in garments of gray or blue . . .

28
00:04:13.821 --> 00:04:17.322
The odor of the dead bodies filled the air."

29
00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:26.151
Famous now, Tolstoy could have enjoyed a life of celebrity, but he discovered within himself a strict moral conscience.

30
00:04:26.152 --> 00:04:36.590
The seeming indifference of the army officers to the awful experiences of ordinary foot soldiers during the war had given him a dislike of authority.

31
00:04:36.591 --> 00:04:48.762
He could not help but compare the peasants fighting beside him to the ones on his estate, and he came to believe that their simple lives were the only ones with true value or meaning.

32
00:04:48.763 --> 00:04:55.451
Upon returning home, Tolstoy decided to create a school for the serfs working at Yasnaya Polyana.

33
00:04:55.452 --> 00:05:04.061
News about Tolstoy's school quickly spread. Some people wondered why the great Tolstoy would bother to educate peasants.

34
00:05:04.062 --> 00:05:12.603
He answered his critics by writing an article arguing that even eminent artists had much to learn from peasants and children.

35
00:05:12.604 --> 00:05:20.143
A restless man, Tolstoy traveled frequently, and wherever he went, he was greeted as a luminary.

36
00:05:20.144 --> 00:05:26.817
But he felt uncomfortable with fame and disliked people who considered themselves better than others.

37
00:05:26.818 --> 00:05:36.541
During a trip to Moscow in 1862, Tolstoy met Sofia Behrs, the daughter of a wealthy doctor, and they quickly fell in love.

38
00:05:36.542 --> 00:05:44.414
On the eve of their wedding, Tolstoy gave his diaries to his new wife, saying she must learn everything about him.

39
00:05:44.415 --> 00:05:52.074
The Tolstoys were married for 48 years and had 13 children, 10 of whom survived infancy.

40
00:05:52.075 --> 00:06:04.418
Sofia managed the household, cared for the children, and acted as secretary to Tolstoy, spending long hours copying out his many scrawled manuscripts in her neat handwriting.

41
00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:15.009
Between 1865 and 1869, Tolstoy wrote War and Peace, considered one of the world's greatest works of literature.

42
00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:31.932
Set in the years 1805 to 1813, the novel tells the stories of five aristocratic Russian families during the Napoleonic Wars, European conflicts that were a result of Napoleon Bonaparte's desire for a French empire.

43
00:06:31.933 --> 00:06:55.371
In this book, Tolstoy explored two important themes: first, that history is created not just by great events or important people, but rather, by millions of ordinary people making thousands of decisions; and second, that free will, according to Tolstoy, is an illusion since everything is ordained by God.

44
00:06:55.372 --> 00:07:02.021
Anna Karenina, published in 1877, was regarded as another masterpiece.

45
00:07:02.022 --> 00:07:11.562
Tolstoy believed that the novel, about a love affair between a married woman and a dashing military officer, was the best thing he had ever written.

46
00:07:11.563 --> 00:07:22.613
He had come to despise the cruelty of the wealthy and powerful, and he rejected the authority of both the government and society—themes he discussed in the book.

47
00:07:22.614 --> 00:07:32.442
After War and Peace and Anna Karenina were published, the world recognized Tolstoy as a literary giant, and he could have rested on his fame.

48
00:07:32.443 --> 00:07:38.202
But his interest lay in finding spiritual and moral truth in his own life.

49
00:07:38.203 --> 00:07:48.698
Over the years, he had come to believe that the Russian Orthodox Church was corrupt and that all organized Christianity misrepresented Christ's message.

50
00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:57.309
He wrote numerous essays on his spiritual beliefs, and people came from around the world to visit his estate and discuss his ideas.

51
00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:09.918
His wife expressed unhappiness at Yasnaya Polyana being overrun by strangers, while Tolstoy struggled daily with the desire to give away all his money and live his life in a simple way.

52
00:08:09.919 --> 00:08:15.471
Sofia strongly opposed this idea because she needed money to raise their children.

53
00:08:15.472 --> 00:08:20.405
Tension that would never soften arose between husband and wife.

54
00:08:20.406 --> 00:08:29.177
"We were what stood in the way of his carrying out his dream of a free, new life," Sofia wrote in her autobiography.

55
00:08:29.178 --> 00:08:41.713
In November 1910, the 82-year-old Tolstoy turned his possessions and land over to his family and set out to finally live the life of an ordinary peasant.

56
00:08:41.714 --> 00:08:44.258
But he did not get very far.

57
00:08:44.259 --> 00:08:52.995
Within days, he fell ill, and the world-famous novelist died in a railroad stationmaster's humble shack.

58
00:08:52.996 --> 00:09:09.312
Because of his relentless search for life's meaning and his desire to shun his wealth, Tolstoy was misunderstood by many of his colleagues, but they could not deny that the writer had found much wisdom in the daily life he observed around him.

59
00:09:09.313 --> 00:09:19.861
Tolstoy's novels and short stories are regarded as among the best, most realistic descriptions of the complex lives of human beings to have ever been written.

60
00:09:19.862 --> 00:09:28.238
As the writer Isaac Babel said, "If the world could write by itself, it would write like Tolstoy."
